<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lancashire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lancashire is a county in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> that became the centre of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> textile industry during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>. Its mills processed raw cotton imported from overseas and exported finished cloth across the world, including to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The industry tied Lancashire closely to the trade networks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lancashire.jpg" alt="Lancashire" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter McDermott · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clitheroe_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4067941.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The economic relationship with India was especially consequential: Indian revenues paid for the Indian Army — the empire's largest standing force — while India served both as a major market for <b>Lancashire</b> textiles and as the keystone of Britain's position in the Indian Ocean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome recruited Sarmatian horsemen, most famously the 5,500 Iazyges sent to Britain in 175 CE, and an inscription from Ribchester in <b>Lancashire</b> records a unit of Sarmatian cavalry still stationed there in the third century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sarmatians">Sarmatians · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Cotton textiles in <b>Lancashire</b>, coal, iron and engineering, and from 1830 the railways, beginning with the Liverpool and Manchester Railway, made Britain the ‘workshop of the world’, as displayed at the Great Exhibition of 1851.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1811 and 1816 the Luddites of Nottinghamshire, Yorkshire and <b>Lancashire</b> smashed machinery, and in 1812 Parliament made the breaking of stocking frames punishable by death.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Stockingers had taken organised action at various times since 1675, and new cotton-spinning technologies in <b>Lancashire</b>, which could be worked by less-skilled, low-wage labourers, met violent resistance in 1768 and 1779.</blockquote><a href="/movement/luddites">Luddites · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Since the 1970s historians have recovered the working-class radical suffragists of the <b>Lancashire</b> cotton towns, the campaigns of African American women and the racism they met within the movement, and the international networks that linked suffragists across empires and oceans.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
